  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nIssued 127 PM MST SUN NOV 22 2026\nA strong upper low off the coast of British Columbia stalls for\nthe early part of the long term forecast. Despite the upper \nlow stalling, a surface low collides with the coast. Initially \nthe low produces efficient precipitation over the Cascades, but \nwith the upper low stalled: the surface low loses steam as it \narrives to our forecast area. A weak shortwave is all that \nremains when it moves overhead Wednesday evening, producing \nlight isolated precipitation if anything. This initial slow \nprogression will allow a high pressure ridge to build up through\nthe Great Basin. By Thursday through Saturday that high \npressure brings temps up to 5-10 degrees above normal and allows\nsnow levels to climb up to 6-8 kft MSL. Just as that ridge \ndevelops, the upper low is finally ready to move east along the \nUS-Canada border. Widespread precipitation is expected late \nThursday through early Saturday, falling almost entirely as rain\nexcept for mountains above 6kft MSL. The best chance for snow \nin mountain valleys will be in the earliest hours of the precip \nThursday evening before snow levels rise. Additionally, the \nzonal flow between the low and the ridge will support gusty \nconditions in open areas and ridgelines. There is still some \nuncertainty on where exactly the precipitation tracks late in \nthe week, with a few ensemble members showing an amplified ridge\npushing precip north. If this high pressure effectively keeps \nmost of the moisture and zonal flow to the north, inversion \nconcerns with low stratus and fog in the Snake River Valley \ncould return by late week.\n\nForecast guidance becomes increasingly messy by the weekend,\nwith several solutions favoring active and unsettled weather \nwith lingering precipitation. All model solutions depict some \nform of another upper level trough or closed low by Sunday, with\nwidely varying storm tracks and impacts for the region. Current\nforecast favors a 30-50% chance of precipitation for lower \nelevations, with better confidence for high terrain in Idaho \nwith a 50-70% chance of measurable precipitation.",
